<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Computer use has reached a turning point: the focus is expanding from GUI-only interaction to systems that complete tasks on users’ computers through visual and programmatic interfaces.
We define a <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalist computer-use agent</em> (GCUA) as a system in which the model chooses how to observe, act, and organize its work across visual and programmatic interfaces.
Yet frontier implementations, such as GPT-6 Astra’s computer-use system, remain largely closed, limiting systematic study of their design.
We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open Generalist CUA</span> (OpenGCUA), an ongoing project to develop and open the full stack for GCUAs with the community.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:2.0pt;">
This report presents the first release of Open Generalist CUA.
<em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Code execution</em> is a key feature of our agent design: it lets intermediate results determine subsequent operations and enables models to apply their existing programming capabilities directly to computer interaction.
We also propose <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness-to-model decision transfer</em>, moving selected observations and task-state decisions from fixed harness rules to the model.
On OSWorld-v2, our full configuration improves task scores across all eight evaluated models, including GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5, raising mean partial score by <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+19.6</span> points and reducing reported steps per task by an average of <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">53.0%</span> relative to batched GUI-only execution.
We release our implementation and prompts, evaluation trajectories from all eight models, and preliminary versions of a <em id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">real-device harness</em> and a <em id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-device SDK</em>.
The harness addresses real-device challenges not fully captured by benchmarks, while the SDK provides a common programming interface across computers, mobiles and browsers.
Across four real macOS tasks under our real-device harness, selected Qwen3.8-Max runs use <span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">19.4</span> times as many reported reasoning tokens as GPT-6 Astra, highlighting substantial differences between models in real-device execution efficiency.</p>
  
</div>
