<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Computer use has moved to the center of attention. The GPT-6 Astra release places strong emphasis on computer use, with extensive demonstrations offering an exciting vision of what computer-use agents could make possible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026c</a>)</cite>.
Much of the literature studies computer-use agents (CUAs) as GUI-only agents, asking how well models can complete computer tasks through screenshot-based observation and mouse and keyboard actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Recent frontier systems, however, combine GUI interaction with shell commands, accessibility-tree access, and other tools, and OpenAI now recommends code execution, writing GUI operations as code and combining them with programmatic operations in a single model output <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026b</a>)</cite>.
These systems also target tasks on users’ own computers, where shared control, interaction with background windows, and evolving requests introduce challenges not fully captured by benchmarks.
The question has changed, from how well a model operates a GUI interface to how well a system completes work on a real computer by whatever means works. We take this as <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a turning point</em> in computer-use research, motivating a clearer definition of these broader agents and a systematic study of their design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore adopt the term <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">generalist computer-use agent</span> (GCUA) to characterize this broader class of systems, following the system-level perspective of Agents’ Last Exam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. GPT-6 Astra’s computer-use system is a representative GCUA. We define a GCUA as <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">an agent system designed to complete diverse computer tasks through both visual interaction and programmatic access, in which the model decides how to observe, act, and organize its work as the task requires</em>. Programmatic access includes shell commands, accessibility APIs, and application or service APIs where available.
Essentially, a GCUA chooses how to use the computer to complete a task, rather than being restricted to GUI interaction. Our previous work, Qwen-UI-Agent, began exploring this direction through a unified GUI and CLI action space with batched actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Within our formulation, such GUI+CLI hybrid CUA is a special case of GCUA (Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">How to design such systems is an open question.
A GCUA raises design questions that go beyond those of GUI-only agents: which interfaces to give the model, how to support code execution, which decisions to leave to the model and which to the harness, and what changes when the agent runs on a user’s own computer.
However, frontier systems such as GPT-6 Astra remain largely closed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026a</a>)</cite>.
Their model weights, full prompting setups, real-device harnesses, device-control SDKs, and execution trajectories are not available.
This limits the community’s ability to reproduce system behavior, compare alternative designs, and investigate how design choices and execution mechanisms affect task completion and cost, particularly under real-device conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore introduce Open Generalist CUA (OpenGCUA), an ongoing project to develop and open the full stack for generalist computer use with the community (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):
<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Model.</span> Develop a model that unifies computer and browser use, with code execution and harness-to-model decision transfer central to its agent design.
<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) System.</span> A real-device harness that runs on users’ real machines, and lessons we learned in moving from benchmark harnesses to real-device deployment. A cross-device SDK that gives agent programs one interface to computers, browsers, and phones, supporting tasks that span multiple devices.
<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Trajectories.</span> Release trajectories from evaluations of frontier models such as GPT-6 Astra to support analysis of code-execution and harness-to-model decision transfer patterns.
<span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Efficiency.</span> Systematically study and improve GCUA execution efficiency through code-execution, concise model reasoning, and harness optimization, while exploring broader system-level improvements.
<span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(5) Evaluation.</span> Develop evaluation environments and benchmarks on real devices, particularly on macOS, to assess agents’ ability to complete tasks under real-world operating conditions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/fig2_v3_candidate_B.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/256;" width="452" height="256" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of Open Generalist CUA.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This report presents the first release of the OpenGCUA project. The release does not yet include a trained model. The report covers agent design and evaluation, execution trajectories from frontier models, and preliminary versions of the real-device harness and cross-device SDK, together with a roadmap for subsequent releases. Our main contributions are as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GCUA generalizes existing GUI, CLI, and hybrid agents.</span>
We define a GCUA by three decisions that its model makes at each step: what to observe, how to act, and how to organize its work. We show that GUI agents, CLI agents, multi-agent hybrids, and GUI+CLI agents such as Qwen-UI-Agent each fix some of these decisions in advance, and are special cases of GCUA (Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GCUA outperforms GUI-only CUA on every frontier model we test.</span>
We implement our GCUA agent design into the OpenGCUA harness, which lets existing models use code execution and harness-to-model decision transfer. We test it with eight frontier models, including GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5. On the offline set of OSWorld-v2, the full GCUA setting raises the mean partial score by <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">19.6 points</span>, cuts steps per task by <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">53.0%</span>, and narrows the score gap between the strongest and weakest models by more than half. We release the implementation, prompts, and <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">evaluation trajectories of all eight frontier models</span> (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code execution adds value beyond shell access.</span>
With the same GUI and shell access, writing GUI operations as code and combining them with programmatic operations adds <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.1 points</span> to the mean partial score and cuts steps per task by <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">14.8%</span> against the GUI+CLI baseline.
Code execution offers two advantages: intermediate results can decide the next operation without another model call, and models can bring their existing programming ability directly to computer interaction. We further identify five recurring patterns of code execution in our case study (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Code Execution over GUI and CLI ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness control should move into the model as models improve.</span>
Harnesses fix many workflow decisions by rule, such as taking a screenshot after every step. We propose <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness-to-model decision transfer</em>, which delegates these harness decisions to the model. In the current implementation, the model decides when to request screenshots and how to track task state. It also states each program’s purpose before execution. On-demand observation alone increases cache hit rates by 10.5 and 14.4 percentage points for two models without lowering task scores (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On real devices, a large gap lies in the model.</span>
We release a preliminary <em id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">real-device harness</em> for users’ own computers, addressing challenges unseen in benchmarks, such as sharing the machine with its user and acting on background windows. With the harness held fixed, Qwen3.8-Max uses <span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">19.4 times</span> as many reasoning tokens as GPT-6 Astra on four real macOS tasks, and <span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.7 times</span> as many output tokens in total. Case studies trace this gap to model behavior: large reasoning cost for simple operations, difficulty adapting to real-device conditions and a gap in code execution capability (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One Programming Interface for Computers, Browsers, and Phones.</span>
The preliminary <em id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cross-Device SDK</em> binds every operation to an explicit device, application, window, or tab, keeps accessibility and vision as separate ways to observe and act, and allows one program to coordinate several devices within a task (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Cross-Device SDK ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This release does not mark the project’s completion, and the harness and SDK remain preliminary.
Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Roadmap ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> outlines our research plans and release milestones for Open Generalist CUA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Definition</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agents’ Last Exam takes a system-level view and decomposes an agent into five layers: the model (Brain), GUI perception (Eyes), orchestration (Body), tools (Hands), and a runtime (Feet) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Under this view, a CLI agent lacks GUI perception, a GUI agent lacks most of the remaining layers, and a generalist agent spans all five.
Having all five layers, however, only means an agent can act through both the GUI and the CLI; it does not mean that the agent chooses well between them.
At a given step, an agent may click through a dozen screens to do what a single shell command would do, or run a script when it first needs to look at the screen. The layer view cannot tell such an agent apart from one that picks the right way to act at each step.
In this report, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">we therefore define a generalist CUA (GCUA) by the decisions it makes at each step, not by the components it contains</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A GCUA makes three decisions at each step: what to observe, how to act, and how to organize.</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What to Observe.</span> The observation can be a screenshot, an accessibility tree (AX Tree), the output of a command, the response of an API, or any combination of these. The model decides which it needs, including whether it needs a new screenshot at all.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How to Act.</span> The action can be a GUI operation at a screen coordinate, an operation on an accessibility element, a shell command, an API call, or a program that combines several of these. The model decides which fits the step.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How to Organize.</span> Across steps, the agent keeps a plan, tracks what has been done, and decides which past observations stay in context. Most harnesses fix these as rules set by the developer. In a GCUA, the model makes these decisions as the task requires.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Four Interfaces.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first two decisions range over four interfaces to the computer, and each offers both a way to observe and a way to act.</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GUI.</span> The agent sees a screenshot and clicks, drags, scrolls, or types at a screen coordinate. This is the interface that always works, since every application has pixels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accessibility Tree.</span> The agent reads the elements of a window, with their roles, names, and values, and operates on an element directly. It needs no coordinate, does not bring the window to the front, and never moves the user’s pointer on real devices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLI.</span> The agent runs shell commands and reads their output. This is the natural route for files, data, and software.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">API.</span> The agent calls external services, such as web search, and reads their responses. This reaches what neither the screen nor the shell can.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each interface offers both a way to observe and a way to act, and each has its own strength. No single interface is best for every step, so what matters is choosing the right one at each step and combining them when a step needs more than one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Putting these together yields the definition used throughout the report: <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">A GCUA is an agent system designed to complete diverse computer tasks through both visual interaction and programmatic access, in which the model decides how to observe, act, and organize its work as the task requires</em>.
This definition refines that of ALE rather than replacing it. Visual interaction and programmatic access require all five layers, and our definition further asks who makes the decisions within them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Formalization.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the state of the computer at step <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the agent’s working state, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the observation returned by the previous step, with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> containing the task instruction. At each step, a GCUA policy <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the three decisions jointly:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(q_{t},\ p_{t},\ m_{t+1})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid m_{t},\ o_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{t},\ p_{t},\ m_{t+1})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid m_{t},\ o_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What to observe:</em> CLI/API output is returned automatically. The model selects additional views through <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{Q}=\{\text{screenshot},\text{AX tree}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>screenshot</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>AX tree</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{Q}=\{\text{screenshot},\text{AX tree}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> means that no additional view is requested.
<em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How to act:</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a program whose statements call primitives in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{GUI}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{AX}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{CLI}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{API}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>GUI</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>AX</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>CLI</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>API</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{GUI}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{AX}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{CLI}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{API}}</annotation></semantics></math> and may use loops, branches, and variables. A single primitive call is the simplest program.
<em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How to organize its work:</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the updated working state, including the plan, the record of progress, and the observations kept in context.
Executing <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> moves the computer to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and produces an execution output <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as command output or API responses. The next observation is</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t+1}=\bigl(e_{t},\ \{v_{q}(s_{t+1})\}_{q\in q_{t}}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t+1}=\bigl(e_{t},\ \{v_{q}(s_{t+1})\}_{q\in q_{t}}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> renders view <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of the computer state.
The model therefore observes both the views it requests through <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as a screenshot or an accessibility tree, and what its program prints in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A Special Case: GUI+CLI Agents.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our recent GUI+CLI agent, Qwen-UI-Agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, released shortly before GPT-6 Astra, is an instructive special case of Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Formalization. ‣ 2.1 Definition ‣ 2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). It places GUI and CLI actions in one action space and lets the model emit a batch of several actions in one call. In our notation, it fixes</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\equiv\{\text{screenshot}\},\qquad p_{t}=(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}),\ a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{GUI}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{CLI}},\qquad m_{t+1}=h(m_{t},o_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>screenshot</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>GUI</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>CLI</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\equiv\{\text{screenshot}\},\qquad p_{t}=(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}),\ a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{GUI}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{CLI}},\qquad m_{t+1}=h(m_{t},o_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed harness rule, such as keeping the last <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> screenshots. The model already chooses, action by action, whether to click or to run a command, and this alone improved both success and efficiency over GUI-only execution.
GCUA lifts each of these three constraints. Where the GUI+CLI agent emits a fixed sequence <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, a GCUA writes a program over the full set <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, which adds accessibility and API operations as well as loops, branches, and variables. Inside such a program, a click, an accessibility operation, a shell command, and an API call are simply four kinds of statements. Where the GUI+CLI agent receives a screenshot after every step, a GCUA decides which views to request, including none at all. And where the GUI+CLI agent follows the harness rule <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, a GCUA decides for itself what to keep in its working state.
In our formulation, batched GUI+CLI hybrid agents are a restricted case of GCUA.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Relation to Existing Agents</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The formalization in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Formalization. ‣ 2.1 Definition ‣ 2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) also lets us compare GCUA with existing agents. Each existing family can be written in the same form, except that some of the three decisions (what to observe, how to act, and how to organize) are fixed in advance instead of being made by the model at each step. Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Relation to Existing Agents ‣ 2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> lists what each family fixes, and the paragraphs below discuss each family in turn.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Existing agent families and GCUA compared on the three decisions. Gray text gives the formal notation. The observation after each step contains the views selected by <math id="S2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the execution output <math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Formalization. ‣ 2.1 Definition ‣ 2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Existing families fix some of these decisions in advance. The shaded rows mark the path this report follows, from our earlier GUI+CLI agent to GCUA, in which the model makes all three decisions at each step.</figcaption>
