<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GUI Agents.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A GUI agent fixes all three decisions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. It always observes a screenshot, so <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\equiv\{\text{screenshot}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>screenshot</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\equiv\{\text{screenshot}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It acts through one GUI operation per step, so <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a single primitive in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{GUI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>GUI</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{GUI}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its working state follows a harness rule <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t+1}=h(m_{t},o_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t+1}=h(m_{t},o_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, such as keeping the last <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> screenshots. The model still chooses where to click, but not what to observe, which interface to use, or what to keep.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CLI Agents.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A CLI agent fixes what it observes and restricts how it acts. It has no view of the screen, so <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\equiv\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\equiv\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and it sees only the execution output <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. It acts only through the shell, so <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a program over <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{CLI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>CLI</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{CLI}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is more flexible than a GUI agent. Its commands and scripts can use loops, branches, and variables. Recent coding agents also let the model decide part of how it organizes its work, for example by summarizing earlier context when it grows long <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>. What a CLI agent cannot do is operate an application that can only be used through its graphical interface. Agent frameworks such as OpenClaw support more than the shell, but they also come without built-in GUI control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Agent Hybrid Systems.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some recent systems combine GUI and programmatic control by dividing the work among several agents. For example, an orchestrator splits a task into subtasks and sends each one to either a GUI operator or a programmer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Each sub-agent has its own fixed interface. The GUI operator sees screenshots and acts through GUI operations, and the programmer writes and runs code. In terms of Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Formalization. ‣ 2.1 Definition ‣ 2 Generalist CUA: Definition and Positioning ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), while sub-agent <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> handles a subtask, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are limited to its own sets <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Together, the sub-agents cover the same interfaces as a GCUA. The difference is where the choice of interface is made. In these systems, the orchestrator makes it once per subtask. In a GCUA, a single model makes it at every step, and one program can combine several interfaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Why Now: The Route Flip to Code Execution</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In 2026, the frontier began to shift from clicking toward programming, and the shift is visible in vendor documentation rather than only in benchmark scores.
With the release of GPT-6 Astra, OpenAI’s computer-use guide began recommending that models write and run short programs, for example with PyAutoGUI or Playwright, rather than rely primarily on the earlier computer tool for mouse and keyboard actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026b</a>)</cite>. The guide’s examples reflect this shift: the computer tool still targets the previous model (i.e., GPT-5.6 Sol), while code-execution examples use GPT-6 Astra. A similar pattern appears on OSWorld-v2, where frontier agents perform more actions through code, APIs, and file operations rather than clicks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
OpenAI also reports that GPT-6 Astra completes OSWorld 2.0 tasks in about 47% less time than GPT-5.6 Sol and is 1.9<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster on Mind2Web <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026c</a>)</cite>.
OpenAI does not say how much of this gain comes from code execution, but it is the kind of gain one would expect when each model call does more work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code Execution versus Batched Actions.</span> With batched actions, each model call yields a list of actions that were fixed when the call was generated: the model must decide every click before it sees the result of the first one. With code execution, each model call yields a program, and control flow is decided while the program runs. Three things become possible in a single call that were impossible before: a <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">condition</em> (<span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">if the dialog is visible, dismiss it</span>), a <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loop</em> (<span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">for each row, fill the three fields</span>), and a <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">verification</em> (<span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert the status now reads Saved</span>). A task that took a dozen round trips becomes one. This is also why batched actions, which our earlier work introduced <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, are a stepping stone rather than an endpoint: a batch is a program without control flow.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Efficiency as a First-Class Metric</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Success rate remains our primary measure, but it does not capture the resources required to complete a task.
For interactive tasks, an important threshold is reached when delegation delivers results sooner than manual execution, potentially saving both human effort and time to completion.
We therefore evaluate efficiency using four metrics: steps per task, output tokens, reasoning tokens, and cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each step is one model call, which may generate multiple actions through batching or code execution.
Fewer steps reduce model-call overhead, while fewer output tokens can lower generation time and cost.
Reasoning tokens are included in output tokens but reported separately.
When reasoning accounts for a large share of output, reducing unnecessary reasoning can improve efficiency without shortening the executable code, provided task performance is maintained.
We compute cost from input and output token usage at each model’s published prices, including cache discounts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section studies two behaviors we aim to develop in GCUA models. The first is <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">code execution</span>, which concerns how to act: the model combines graphical interaction and programmatic operations within a single program. The second is <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">harness-to-model decision transfer</span>, which concerns what to observe and how to organize: the model makes selected workflow control decisions that a harness would otherwise fix with rules, such as when to look at the screen and how to organize task state. This release does not introduce a trained model. Instead, we study how the prompts, tool interfaces, and execution runtime of Open Generalist CUA support these behaviors in existing frontier models. We first explain the design and illustrate it with execution trajectories, then evaluate its effects on task completion and cost.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Code Execution over GUI and CLI</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Code execution gives graphical interaction and programmatic operations a unified executable form. The model writes a program in which GUI actions, such as <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pyautogui.click()</span>, can be combined with shell commands and Python computation. The model can therefore specify not only what operations to perform, but also how execution should proceed. Loops and conditional logic allow the program to repeat operations and choose subsequent actions based on intermediate results, without requiring a new model call for each operation. On OSWorld-v2, this design improves Qwen3.8-Max’s mean task score by 5.2 points and reduces steps per task by 17.0% relative to a hybrid CUA baseline with batched GUI+CLI actions (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Experiments on Agent Design ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Implementation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Design.</span>
The system prompt specifies the programming interfaces and output format for code execution. The OSWorld-v2 configuration specifies PyAutoGUI for desktop interaction and the Python function <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bash()</span> for executing shell commands.
The prompt for the real-device harness additionally describes Playwright for browser interaction. The prompt requires one Python program in a <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;code&gt;</span> block per step and includes examples that combine interface operations, shell commands, and checks on intermediate results. The full prompt is given in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompts ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Program Execution.</span>
The harness extracts and syntax-checks the code block, then executes it in the computer environment. The runtime supports desktop interaction through PyAutoGUI and, in the real-device harness, browser interaction through Playwright.
Shell commands execute through <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bash()</span>, which returns command output and exit status. The Python control flow coordinates these calls, allowing loops and conditions to combine GUI and shell operations within one program.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution Feedback.</span>
The harness returns captured standard output, standard error, and execution status to the model. With on-demand observation enabled, observations include a new screenshot when the program requests one through the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">screenshot()</span> action. The real-device harness additionally returns accessibility-tree observations when requested by the model.
We have open-sourced both the preliminary real-device harness and the code execution harness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Representative Code-Execution Patterns</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The implementation specifies what a program can contain, but we want to see how models actually write programs to solve concrete tasks.
We therefore examine GPT-6 Astra on OSWorld-v2, and find five recurring patterns in how its programs connect actions, intermediate results, and model observations. The examples illustrate complementary uses of code, rather than mutually exclusive categories or isolated performance gains.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/patterns_execution_v2.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Programs coordinate actions and checks.</span>
(a) GUI clicks alternate with checks of generated contracts and local drafts.
(b) A loop visits seven webpage sections and checks each active section through the DOM.
(c) A draft comparison determines whether to click Send, without verifying delivery.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/patterns_evidence_v3.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/226;" width="476" height="226" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Programs prepare and verify evidence.</span>
(a) A program assembles 25 views into a contact sheet for the model’s next observation.
(b) Assertions check background images and transparency, and verify that non-background content remains unchanged.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Interface Composition.</span>
A single program can use information from one interface to guide operations through another.
A program can alternate between GUI actions and programmatic checks within one model step. In a contract-generation task, the agent clicks a spreadsheet button, reads the application’s completion status, and checks the output files. Only after these checks pass does it click a second button to create local email drafts, which it then inspects programmatically. This example connects GUI execution with structured feedback from both the application and its files (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1.2 Representative Code-Execution Patterns ‣ 3.1 Code Execution over GUI and CLI ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loop-Based Execution.</span>
Loops let the model specify a procedure once and repeat it across multiple targets within one program. Unlike a fixed action list, the targets and number of iterations can be determined during execution, for example from file contents or application state. This allows repeated work to proceed without generating a separate action sequence or making a new model call for each iteration.
To record a website walkthrough, the agent iterates over seven navigation targets, clicks each target, and reads the active section from the DOM. An assertion checks that the resulting section matches the intended target before the loop continues. The program handles all seven visits and checks without returning to the model between iterations (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1.2 Representative Code-Execution Patterns ‣ 3.1 Code Execution over GUI and CLI ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result-Dependent Execution.</span>
Conditional logic lets the program respond to what happens during execution. It can inspect command output or application state, check a result, and decide whether to continue. Such decisions can remain within the program when their conditions are computable, rather than requiring the model to interpret each intermediate result. A local check can determine whether a GUI action should proceed. Before sending an email reply, the agent copies the draft from the interface and compares it with a saved version after trimming leading and trailing whitespace. Its program clicks Send only if the texts match and reports a mismatch otherwise. The recorded execution follows the matching branch, but delivery is not verified within this step (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1.2 Representative Code-Execution Patterns ‣ 3.1 Code Execution over GUI and CLI ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>c).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation Preparation.</span>
Programs can gather and organize evidence for the next model decision. They can collect multiple views, crop relevant regions, or arrange images into a visual summary. This extends code execution from performing actions to preparing observations that the model can then interpret. While inspecting a three-dimensional object, the agent collects 25 views, crops the relevant region from each, and assembles a numbered contact sheet. It opens this sheet and requests one new visual observation. The program collects and arranges views for the model’s visual judgment (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1.2 Representative Code-Execution Patterns ‣ 3.1 Code Execution over GUI and CLI ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Executable Verification.</span>
Programs can check both intended changes and content that should remain unchanged. After editing two slide backgrounds, the agent compares the original and saved presentation packages. It checks the expected changes, verifies that the other 64 original package entries are unchanged, and compares slide content outside the background elements. These assertions make selected preservation requirements explicit, but do not replace independent evaluation (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1.2 Representative Code-Execution Patterns ‣ 3.1 Code Execution over GUI and CLI ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">These examples show a useful division of work: programs execute locally specified operations and checks, while the model chooses the procedure and interprets evidence that still requires judgment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3 </span>Limits of Visual-Only Feedback</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Result-dependent control is harder when interface observations are limited to screenshots. Conditions such as whether a button is enabled or an input field contains the expected value are not directly exposed as program-readable values, so checking them may require another model call. Accessibility trees can expose text and control states as structured data, allowing programs to query supported interface properties and use them in loops and conditional logic. This motivates combining visual observation with accessibility-tree access in our real-device harness design.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Code execution addresses <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how to act</em> by allowing the model to express computer operations as programs. A GCUA must also decide <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what to observe</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how to organize</em> its work. We study <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harness-to-model decision transfer</em>: replacing selected fixed harness rules with task-dependent model decisions. A screenshot, for example, may be necessary after a GUI action but redundant when program output already answers the relevant question. Deciding whether to request it should depend on the information needed to continue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mechanism in the Harness, Decision in the Model.</span>
As models become more capable, we envision progressively shifting selected workflow decisions from fixed harness rules to the model. The harness should provide reliable mechanisms, while the model decides when and how to use them based on the task and available evidence. In the current implementation, screenshot requests and task-state updates are model-initiated workflow operations, complemented by model-generated action-intent annotations. The harness continues to execute interface calls, preserve task records, and manage context retention.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Mechanisms for Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On-Demand Observation.</span>
The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">screenshot</span> function lets the model decide whether its next input needs a new screenshot. It can request visual feedback after a GUI action or continue without it when text output is sufficient. On-demand observation can reduce context length by omitting screenshots that add little information. The environment still captures a screenshot after every step, so the mechanism changes what the model receives, not what the environment records.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-State Tracking.</span>
The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">todo</span> interface lets the model maintain a persistent record of progress, facts, and unresolved questions. It updates this record through <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">todo.update()</span> and retrieves it through <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">todo.list()</span>.
Statuses distinguish pending work, attempted changes, unverified changes, and claimed verification, helping the model track what still needs checking. These entries record the model’s assessment rather than independently verifying the environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action Intent.</span>
The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">intent</span> annotation makes the purpose of each code-execution step explicit. Before each program runs, the model provides a short <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;intent&gt;</span> block stating what the code is intended to accomplish. A program may combine many operations, making its overall purpose difficult to infer from the code alone.
In real-device use, the harness can display this description to users, helping them understand what the agent is trying to do at each step without reading the program.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness-Managed Context.</span>
The harness manages historical tool outputs and screenshots through two fixed policies. A <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rolling screenshot buffer</span> removes older images in batches, limiting visual context while reducing disruption to prompt caching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026a</a>)</cite>. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Micro-compaction</span> applies a similar rolling-buffer policy to textual tool outputs, replacing older outputs with short placeholders in batches while retaining recent results. These retention decisions remain with the harness, complementing the model’s choices about when to obtain new observations and how to track task progress.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Representative Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer Patterns</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2.2 Representative Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer Patterns ‣ 3.2 Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows these interfaces in model outputs. The examples come from GPT-6 Astra trajectories on OSWorld-v2. They illustrate the mechanisms, not their individual effects on task success.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Requesting Visual Feedback Only When Needed.</span>
In a calendar task, the model checks saved events and calls <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">screenshot()</span>. Its next input contains text output and a new screenshot. The following program inspects event styles without requesting another image, so the next input contains text feedback but no new screenshot. These adjacent steps show the observation decision: the model can request visual input or continue with programmatic feedback. Earlier screenshots may still remain in context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keeping Unfinished Work Explicit across Programs.</span>
In a form-filling task, the model records that all fields have been entered but submission is still pending. It marks the task <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">changed_unverified</span>. After a later program clicks Submit and reads the page’s confirmation, the model updates the same record to <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">claimed_verified</span>. The persistent record distinguishes work performed from an outcome checked. Its status remains the model’s assessment, not an independent evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stating a Program’s Purpose before Execution.</span>
In a CRM task, one intent statement summarizes a 64-line program: update 29 lead statuses while preserving the original customer details. The program performs status-only updates and checks other fields, allowing modification timestamps to change. The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;intent&gt;</span> block makes both the intended change and the preservation requirement explicit without requiring the reader to reconstruct them from the code.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/workflow_patterns_compact_v2.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model outputs for observation, task state, and intent.</span>
(a) The two programs differ in whether they request a new screenshot for the next input.
(b) A form record remains unverified until the model reads submission confirmation.
(c) Intent states what the program should change and preserve.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Experiments on Agent Design</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether the agent design presented in this report helps frontier models complete computer tasks more effectively. We first compare the full GCUA configuration with a GUI-only baseline that already supports batched actions. We then separate the effects of interface access, code execution, and harness-to-model decision transfer.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Models and Configuration</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark and Models.</span>
Following the evaluation protocol of the GPT-6 Astra release <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026c</a>)</cite>, we evaluate on the 82-task offline subset of OSWorld-v2 (v2.1) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, which excludes the 26 tasks that require internet access and so keeps results independent of external websites at run time. The main comparison covers eight models: Claude Opus 5, GPT-6 Astra, Gemini 3.8 Flash, Qwen3.8-Max, Qwen3.8-Flash, Kimi K3, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, and GLM-5.3-Flash.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution Configurations.</span>
The CUA baseline uses GUI-only interaction with batched actions enabled. The full Open Generalist CUA (<span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenGCUA</span>) configuration combines code execution over GUI and shell operations with the model-initiated workflow control and harness-managed context policies described above. For the ablations of code execution, harness-to-model decision transfer, and harness-managed context policies, we explicitly specify which components are enabled in each setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We evaluate task performance and execution efficiency. Following OSWorld-v2, we report binary success and partial-credit scores to measure full and partial task completion, respectively. We measure efficiency by mean steps per task and mean output tokens per task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>CUA versus GCUA</h4>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
