<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the conventional GUI-only CUA setting with the full OpenGCUA configuration to examine the performance and efficiency gains from moving toward generalist computer use. OpenGCUA gives the model greater control over what to observe, how to act, and how to organize its work. The CUA baseline already supports batched actions, while OpenGCUA combines code execution with harness-to-model decision transfer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenGCUA improves task scores and reduces steps across all eight models.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3.2 CUA versus GCUA ‣ 3.3 Experiments on Agent Design ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the cross-model mean partial score increases from 45.3 to 64.9, a gain of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">19.6 points</span>, while steps per task decrease by an average of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">53.0%</span>, computed from each model’s relative reduction. The largest score gains reach <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">35.2 points</span> for GLM-5.3-Flash and <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">29.8 points</span> for DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash. Full-task success also increases for every model. These results show a substantial gap between batched GUI-only execution and the full OpenGCUA design in both task performance and interaction efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The gains hold despite large differences in baseline performance and efficiency.</span> The benefits are not limited to models with low initial task scores. GPT-6 Astra, which has the highest baseline score, improves from 67.1 to 75.7 and reduces steps per task from 102.1 to 33.5, a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">67.2%</span> reduction. At the other end of the efficiency range, Qwen3.8-Flash starts with 232.0 steps per task, yet gains <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">21.6 points</span> while using <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">62.8%</span> fewer steps. The consistent improvements across all 8 frontier models support the broad applicability of the OpenGCUA design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fewer interaction steps do not necessarily require fewer output tokens.</span>
Output tokens per task decrease for six of the eight models, with an average relative reduction of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">27.2%</span>. Claude Opus 5 reduces its output from 378.3K to 89.3K tokens per task, and Kimi K3 from 302.5K to 139.6K. GPT-6 Astra, however, uses approximately the same output-token budget, despite its large reduction in steps. Fewer steps per task can reduce model round trips and their overhead, even without reducing output tokens. End-to-end latency must be measured separately, accounting for model response time and tool execution time. We report both steps and output tokens to distinguish interaction efficiency from output-token usage.</p>
</div>
