<div id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On-demand observation improves cache reuse.</span>
The <span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">screenshot</span> action lets the model omit screenshots when program output is sufficient, aiming to reduce unnecessary visual context. In the selected runs, cache hit rises from 75.6% to 86.1% for Qwen3.8-Max and from 61.9% to 76.3% for DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, with higher task scores in both cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-state tracking and action intent serve distinct goals.</span>
The <span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">todo</span> interface supports task completion by maintaining a record of progress and unresolved work. Task score increases from 62.4 to 64.0 for Qwen3.8-Max and from 56.7 to 58.5 for DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash. In contrast, the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">intent</span> annotation makes each program’s purpose explicit so that real-device users can understand what the agent is doing. Its primary goal is user understanding rather than benchmark performance. Scores fall slightly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cache reuse and context length matter for practical agent execution.</span>
As interaction histories grow, context policies must control their length while preserving cache reuse. The rolling screenshot buffer removes older images in batches, reducing how often prior context is modified. In the selected runs, cache hit improves by <span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16.0 and 13.4 percentage points</span> for Qwen3.8-Max and DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, respectively. Micro-compaction instead replaces older tool outputs with short placeholders to limit context growth, at the potential cost of invalidating cached prefixes. Cache hit falls from 75.6% to 68.6% for Qwen3.8-Max, and from 61.9% to 58.9% for DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash. Neither context policy lowers task score in these selected comparisons.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.6 </span>How Much Task Performance Can Money and Time Buy?</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Final benchmark scores alone do not show how much performance is available under limited resources. We therefore examine monetary and time constraints using the same selected full-setting runs as the main evaluation on the offline subset of OSWorld-v2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Measuring Performance under Resource Limits.</span>
We retrospectively apply a common spending cap to every task. A trajectory contributes its recorded final partial score only if its full cost fits within the cap; otherwise, it contributes zero.
We estimate spending by assuming that each attempt ends when its recorded trajectory finishes or its spending cap is reached, whichever comes first. Spending on unsuccessful attempts is also included.
Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.3.6 How Much Task Performance Can Money and Time Buy? ‣ 3.3 Experiments on Agent Design ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>(a) plots the resulting score against average expenditure per task, not the cap itself. Panel (b) applies the same scoring rule to a per-task time limit. Scores are averaged over all tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, recorded wall-clock time depends on API bandwidth and rate limits, the number of concurrently active evaluation environments, provider-side queuing, and tool execution latency. These conditions were not controlled across models, limiting the comparability of the measurements. We therefore report the time curves as exploratory evidence and treat their trends as indicative rather than conclusive.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/OpenGCUA_money_time.png" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/226;" width="452" height="226" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S3.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task performance under monetary and time constraints.</span>
Mean partial scores on the 82-task offline subset of OSWorld-v2 under increasing per-task spending caps (a) and recorded time limits (b).
Panel (a) reports average expenditure, including unsuccessful attempts.
Top bars identify the highest-scoring models; shading indicates cache-write cost bounds.
Time comparisons are exploratory.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Money and Time Favor Different Models.</span>
Under the assumed API prices, the highest-scoring model shifts from Qwen3.8-Flash to GLM-5.3-Flash, Gemini 3.8 Flash, and eventually GPT-6 Astra as the monetary budget increases. For example, Qwen3.8-Flash reaches 60.5 points at approximately $0.38 per task, whereas GPT-6 Astra reaches the highest score of 75.7 at approximately $15.26 per task. In the recorded-time comparison, however, GPT-6 Astra achieves higher scores at most time limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From Benchmark Scores to Resource-Aware Model Selection.</span>
In practice, the relevant question is not only which model achieves the highest benchmark score, but which delivers the most task performance within a given budget or an acceptable completion time. These curves make this distinction explicit: models with lower final scores may be preferable under tighter resource constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Real-Device Generalist CUA</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we study code execution and harness-to-model decision transfer on OSWorld-v2, where every task runs in a controlled benchmark environment. Users, however, run agents on their own computers.
There, the environment is not initialized for the task, many application windows overlap, the user works at the same time, and requests change as the task goes on, so an agent that performs well on a benchmark may still struggle on a real device.
This section first describes how real devices differ from benchmarks (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 The Gap Between Benchmarks and Real Machines ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). It then presents the Cross-Device SDK, the programming interface through which the model operates different devices (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Cross-Device SDK ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), and the harness we built for real devices, organized into five layers with clear responsibilities (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>The Gap Between Benchmarks and Real Machines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Controlled computer-use evaluations typically provide prepared task states, an initial instruction, and an environment reserved for the agent. Such settings simplify evaluation but do not fully capture the demands of operating a user’s computer. We highlight five requirements that become especially important when the agent works alongside a user in an existing, changing environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The user and agent share control.</span>
The user may continue working while the agent runs, so the agent cannot assume exclusive control of the pointer, keyboard, or focused window. It must account for user activity rather than attribute every change to its own actions. Where supported, it should prefer accessibility actions or background-window operations that leave the user’s current interaction undisturbed. When these methods cannot complete an operation, foreground interaction requires coordination with the user before taking control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The screen keeps changing.</span>
Application windows open, close, and move, and one window can cover another. These changes may come from applications unrelated to the task and continue while the agent is reasoning. A coordinate or element reference that was valid when observed may therefore be wrong by the time the agent acts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User requests evolve during execution.</span>
Users may clarify an initial request, correct the agent’s approach, or add constraints as work proceeds. The agent must keep track of the overall task goal and relevant progress across interruptions and turns, while revising its plan to reflect the user’s latest instructions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation and control require explicit targets.</span>
On a user’s computer, the task-relevant application, window, or browser tab need not be in the foreground. A full-desktop screenshot shows the visible composition of windows, not the contents of every addressable target. The model must therefore choose both what to inspect and how to inspect it: a target-specific image, an accessibility tree, or a programmatic query may expose different aspects of the same target. For example, an agent inspecting a background product page may receive an unrelated login page if it requests a desktop screenshot rather than an image of the selected tab, as illustrated in Section <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS4" title="4.3.4 Case Studies ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Observation and control also impose different requirements. Being able to inspect a background target does not imply that every operation on it can run in the background. The model must select a supported control method, while the harness makes target identities, available capabilities, and foreground-input requirements explicit. Observations and actions should remain tied to their selected targets rather than silently following changes in desktop focus. These requirements link the model’s observation and action choices to the resource bindings and capability declarations in the Cross-Device SDK (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Cross-Device SDK ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks begin in an existing user environment.</span>
Benchmark tasks typically start from a prepared state. A user’s computer instead retains files, open tabs, application windows, and account sessions from prior use. Relevant materials may be mixed with unrelated content or similarly named files, requiring the agent to identify the correct inputs before acting. Expired sessions or missing permissions may also require user involvement before execution can continue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">These requirements mean that benchmark scores alone do not establish reliable operation on users’ devices. We therefore study real-device capability alongside benchmark performance, rather than treating deployment as an implementation detail. The following sections describe our SDK and harness, and Section <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS3" title="4.3.3 Preliminary Real-Device Results ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.3</span></a> reports preliminary real-device results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Cross-Device SDK</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A computer-use task may span a mobile application, desktop tools, and a browser-based service. The Cross-Device SDK provides a common programming interface through which one agent can coordinate these environments. It supports both the reuse of common operations across platforms and the composition of cross-device workflows within a single task. Its programming model builds on code-execution-based computer-use interfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/sdk_architecture.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/232;" width="476" height="232" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span><span id="S4.F8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture of the Cross-Device SDK.</span> A common programming interface connects agent programs to computers, mobile devices, and browser environments, enabling cross-device workflows through platform-specific adapters.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shared Operations with Explicit Targets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Common operations use a shared vocabulary, while platform adapters implement their native behavior. For example, the macOS adapter uses the unmodified Cua Driver from trycua <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">trycua, 2026</a>)</cite>. Each operation is scoped to an explicit resource, such as a device, application, window, or browser tab. Resource handles retain their targets independently, so selecting a new target does not redirect earlier bindings. This gives agent programs a consistent way to address multiple devices and applications without relying on a single implicitly selected target.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-Device Coordination within One Task.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within one session, programs can combine observations and actions from multiple resources while reusing host-side variables and bindings across turns. The program executes in the host runtime, whereas <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">device.shell.run</span> executes commands on the selected device where supported. File transfers through <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mu.files.copy</span> identify both source and destination resources. These mechanisms allow intermediate results from one environment to guide operations in another. As an illustrative workflow, an agent could retrieve a report through a phone application, transfer it to a computer for analysis, and enter the results into a browser-based service, coordinating all three stages within the same task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shared Observation Processing and Selective Delivery.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SDK exposes <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ui.ax</span> for structured semantic observations and element-based actions, and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ui.vision</span> for images and coordinate-based interaction. Semantic state supports programmatic conditions and checks, while vision provides complementary information when semantic observations are insufficient. Adapters normalize native accessibility and browser semantic trees into common representations, allowing filtering and compact rendering to be reused across compatible platforms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Observation capture is separate from delivery to the model. Calls such as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ui.ax.observe()</span> return data to the program, while <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mu.emit</span> delivers selected observations or computed results to the model. A program can therefore inspect multiple targets, use structured results to determine subsequent operations, and return only the information needed for the next model decision. This separation supports cross-device execution without requiring a model call for every intermediate structured observation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Explicit Platform Capabilities.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common interface does not imply identical capabilities across platforms. Each resource declares its supported operations and options. Common UI operations and device services share interfaces, while platform-specific features remain explicit through namespaces such as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.android</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.macos</span>, and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.browser</span>. Operations such as Android navigation and browser page evaluation thus retain their own semantics, allowing programs to reuse common interaction patterns while selecting methods appropriate to each target.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Real-Device Harness</h3>

<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/harnesss_architecture.png" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/201;" width="429" height="201" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="S4.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Five-layer architecture of the real-device harness.</span> The runtime executes model-generated programs through the SDK and platform drivers to interact with applications and the operating system.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Cross-Device SDK defines the programming interface for device observation and control. The real-device harness integrates this interface with the agent loop, program execution, and platform-specific mechanisms, enabling the model to work on a user’s computer while retaining task state across turns.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Five-Layer Architecture</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We separate the system into five layers (Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). The model generates a program, the runtime executes it, and SDK calls are translated by the driver into operations on applications and the operating system. Explicit interfaces separate responsibilities and allow compatible components to be replaced independently. Detectable execution failures are reported where they occur; successful action delivery is not treated as evidence that the intended task-level effect has occurred.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model.</span>
Conditioned on the task, system prompt, and interaction history, the model decides how to observe, act, and organize its work. It selects targets and operations, maintains task state, and determines when to request user input. These decisions are expressed as Python programs against a common SDK, allowing device operations and programmatic checks to share the same control flow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime.</span>
The runtime manages the agent loop and executes programs in a persistent interpreter. Variables, imports, and the working directory persist across turns, supporting incremental execution. It collects execution feedback and explicitly emitted observations, applies the configured context-retention policies, and constructs the next model input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Device SDK.</span>
The SDK binds observations and actions to explicit resources, such as devices, windows, and browser tabs (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Cross-Device SDK ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Short element IDs written directly by the model must originate from observations delivered to it; programs may also obtain element references from fresh observations and use them within the same execution. Resource resets or rebinding invalidate associated references. These constraints establish the provenance and scope of action targets, but do not guarantee that the interface remains unchanged before execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Driver.</span>
The driver translates SDK requests into supported native operations, including accessibility actions and coordinate-based input. It manages element identities and snapshot metadata, selects action-delivery mechanisms, and applies foreground or background delivery policies. Detectable native failures are returned as typed errors so that the agent can distinguish execution failures from outcomes requiring further inspection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Applications and Operating System.</span>
Applications and the operating system determine the available observations and control capabilities. Permissions, application state, and accessibility support constrain which operations can be performed. The harness must expose these limitations and use alternative interaction methods where supported.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Responding to Real-Device Challenges</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The constraints discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 The Gap Between Benchmarks and Real Machines ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> motivate four design choices that connect these layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minimizing Interference on Shared Machines.</span>
An agent operating alongside a user cannot assume exclusive control of the pointer, keyboard, or focused window. The harness therefore prefers element-level accessibility actions, followed by window-targeted coordinate input, and finally foreground input. Background delivery is used only where the target and platform support it; window-relative coordinates alone do not imply background execution. When a less intrusive method is unavailable or ineffective, the agent can escalate to a supported alternative, coordinating with the user before foreground input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selecting Observations Explicitly.</span>
A full-desktop screenshot may show the user’s activity rather than the agent’s target, and visual input may be unnecessary during programmatic work. Following the model-initiated observation design in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ 3 Agent Design: Code Execution and Harness-to-Model Decision Transfer ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, the agent chooses when to observe and which target to inspect. A program can capture observations, process structured results, and emit relevant information within one execution; images are interpreted by the model when delivered in a subsequent input. Bounded history and image retention limit accumulated context. These mechanisms reduce unnecessary observations, but do not eliminate the need to refresh state when the interface changes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Managing Accessibility Information.</span>
Accessibility observations vary in size, stability, and coverage across applications. The harness filters irrelevant nodes and represents the retained hierarchy as a flat list linked by <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">parent_id</span>. Element identities and snapshot versions support reference tracking, while <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">page</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">read</span> retrieve relevant information from a retained snapshot without repeatedly capturing the window. When accessibility information is insufficient, visual observation provides a complementary source of evidence. The choice of action-delivery mechanism remains separate from the choice of observation modality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maintaining Task Continuity through User Interaction.</span>
Real-device tasks may require clarification, authentication, or authorization that the agent cannot supply independently. The <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ask_user</span> interface supports these exchanges, including resolving ambiguous targets, requesting assistance with login or verification, and obtaining authorization for consequential actions. User responses and corrections become part of the ongoing task. The runtime preserves interpreter state, resource bindings, and interaction history, allowing execution to resume without rebuilding the workflow.</p>
</div>
