<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3 </span>Preliminary Real-Device Results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As our real-device benchmark is still under development, we study seven model configurations on four macOS tasks: workbook reasoning, PDF form filling, dietary-constrained shopping research, and hotel comparison, with up to three recorded runs per task and model. Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.3.2 Responding to Real-Device Challenges ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes five configurations, reporting mean interaction costs across all four tasks, including incomplete tasks. Steps count model responses, and output tokens include reasoning where recorded. Dollar costs are estimated from recorded response usage, cache hits, and public API prices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution Efficiency.</span>
Generation volume differs more sharply than interaction length. GPT-6 Astra averages 34.5 steps and 12.9K output tokens per task, compared with 50.8 steps and 73.4K tokens for Qwen3.8-Max: <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.5 times as many steps and 5.7 times as many output tokens</span>. Much of this difference lies in reported reasoning, which averages 2.6K tokens for GPT and 50.2K for Qwen. Claude Opus 5 and Gemini 3.8 Flash use 42.3K and 31.9K output tokens per task, respectively. Token usage and monetary cost need not rank models in the same order: under the archived pricing assumptions, Qwen3.8-Max costs $1.49 per task, compared with $18.29 for GPT-6 Astra.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-Level Differences.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the workbook task, both selected runs satisfy the evaluation criteria, but GPT-6 Astra uses 22 steps and 8,783 output tokens, compared with 37 steps and 120,831 tokens for Qwen3.8-Max. Reported reasoning accounts for 1,487 and 92,974 tokens, respectively. On the PDF task, Qwen also uses significantly more output tokens (28,268 versus 10,013). The following case studies examine how observation choices and code execution contribute to the observed differences. With only four tasks and one run per model, these results characterize the evaluated configurations rather than establish a general model ranking.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/realdevice_observation_v2.png" id="S4.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/248;" width="476" height="248" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span><span id="S4.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning cost and observation-target selection.</span>
GPT-6 Astra captures the selected Amazon page, while Qwen3.8-Max captures the foreground Taobao page and then switches to Amazon. Qwen3.8-Max uses substantially more reasoning tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.4 </span>Case Studies</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The gap in real-device execution extends from basic operation to completing an entire task.</span>
We compare GPT-6 Astra and Qwen3.8-Max through earlier low-effort trajectories under the same harness on real-device tasks, separate from the selected runs in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.3.2 Responding to Real-Device Challenges ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The following cases show differences in the cost of routine operations, the selection of observation targets, and the ability to turn a task into an effective program.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Large Differences in Reasoning Cost for Simple Operations.</span>
In a shopping task, Qwen produces a four-line program that captures and returns a desktop screenshot. The program returns an image, but the model response uses 2,105 output tokens, including 2,030 reasoning tokens. GPT also obtains a screenshot in another step of the same task, but its entire response uses only 124 output tokens, including 11 reasoning tokens (Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Task-Level Differences. ‣ 4.3.3 Preliminary Real-Device Results ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). This contrast shows that a substantial reasoning-cost gap can arise even in a simple observation step. Such generation overhead can introduce substantial execution latency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Difficulty Adapting to Real-Device Conditions.</span>
On a real device, the page an agent needs to inspect may not be in the foreground. In the shopping task, Qwen is working with an Amazon browser object but requests a desktop screenshot, which shows the foreground Taobao login page. It then brings Amazon forward and captures again. GPT instead requests a screenshot directly from its selected browser page. Both models use screenshots; the contrast concerns observation scope, illustrating why the intended task target cannot be assumed to coincide with the foreground interface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A Gap in Task-Level Code Execution.</span>
A workbook task reveals a difference in how models translate task requirements into programs. GPT-6 Astra builds a reusable solver, checks it against seven supplied examples, and identifies question rows from the workbook contents. Its program generates answers for all 125 questions. Qwen3.8-Max also uses functions and loops, but processes fixed row ranges that cover only 85 questions. Although its program reports this count, Qwen3.8-Max does not check for omitted questions. An audit of the saved workbooks confirms that GPT fills all 125 required Level answer cells, while Qwen3.8-Max leaves 40 empty (Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.3.4 Case Studies ‣ 4.3 Real-Device Harness ‣ 4 Real-Device Generalist CUA ‣ OpenGCUA: Toward a Fully Open Stack for Generalist Computer Use" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). The gap is therefore not simply whether a model can write executable code with loops and conditions, but whether it can use code correctly to cover the full task and check for incomplete work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.22200v1/realdevice_programs_v2.png" id="S4.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/252;" width="476" height="252" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span><span id="S4.F11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task coverage in code execution.</span>
GPT-6 Astra fills all 125 required Level answer cells, while Qwen3.8-Max leaves 40 empty. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Roadmap</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Open Generalist CUA aims to make generalist computer use a shared research effort in which models, execution systems, and real-device evaluation advance together. This first release provides an initial basis for that work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>From Design Validation to Model Release</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The model-side contribution of this report is <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">empirical validation of agent design using existing models</span>, rather than a newly trained model. We study code execution and harness-to-model decision transfer, and release implementations, prompts, and evaluation trajectories alongside preliminary versions of the real-device harness and cross-device SDK.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A GCUA model is the central addition planned for the next release.</span>
We aim to unify computer and browser use and turn the designs studied here into learned capabilities: coordinating visual and programmatic operations through code, requesting observations when needed, maintaining task state throughout execution, and particularly enhancing the capability in real-device scenario.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Research Priorities</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">More Efficient Reasoning and Execution.</span>
We will optimize the model’s thinking, making reasoning more concise for routine operations and mitigating overthinking. On the harness and infrastructure side, we will improve code execution, context management, and infrastructure optimization. We will also explore new system architectures and other system-level solutions for efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reliable Work on Users’ Devices.</span>
We plan to extend the harness and SDK toward tasks spanning computers, browsers, and potentially mobile devices. Priorities include reliable target selection, recovery from execution failures, and task continuity when users interrupt or change their requests. We will also investigate which additional workflow decisions should move from fixed harness rules to the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-Device Environments and Benchmarks.</span>
We will develop evaluation environments and benchmarks on real devices, particularly macOS, covering a wider range of applications, user interactions, and changing device states. The goal is to measure whether agents complete useful work under real operating conditions, together with the time and resources required.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>How to Contribute</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We welcome contributions to both the implementation and the research agenda:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improve the Harness and SDK.</span> Contribute platform adapters, more reliable observation and control, execution optimizations, or real-device testing and documentation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Build Real-Device Evaluation.</span> Contribute environments, realistic tasks, evaluation criteria, and benchmark infrastructure, including execution traces that expose current limitations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Propose Research Goals.</span> Identify workflows agents should support, capabilities worth developing, or questions to investigate through the released systems and trajectories.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These priorities guide planned releases without fixing a delivery timeline. The agenda will evolve with experimental evidence and community proposals, including directions beyond those listed here. Our goal is for the community to help shape not only how GCUAs are built, but also what they should be able to do and how progress should be measured.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frontier Computer Use.</span> OpenAI positions GPT-6 Astra for difficult end-to-end work and describes computer use as a new frontier in speed, accuracy, and safety. It recommends code execution for computer-use tasks while retaining the structured <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">computer</span> tool as an alternative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026b</a>)</cite>. In Codex, Computer Use can see and operate graphical user interfaces on macOS and Windows, support workflows spanning multiple applications, and, on macOS, run scoped background tasks while the user continues working elsewhere <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026a</a>)</cite>. Anthropic also provides computer use in Claude Cowork and Claude Code: Claude first reaches for the most precise tool, such as connectors to services like Slack or Google Calendar, and can directly control the browser, mouse, keyboard, and screen when no connector is available. Its Dispatch feature further lets users assign tasks from a phone and open the completed work on a computer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026b</a>)</cite>. Despite these capabilities, the public releases leave key implementation details and execution trajectories unavailable, making their system designs difficult for the community to reproduce and study systematically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computer-Use Agents.</span> Prior open CUA models remain largely GUI-centric. UI-TARS and UI-TARS-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, OpenCUA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, ScaleCUA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, EvoCUA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and Fara <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2026</a>)</cite> release open models for computer use and report strong results on relevant benchmarks. Recent work has begun to explore hybrid GUI–programmatic execution: CoAct-1, ComputerRL, UltraCUA, ToolCUA, and StateAct combine GUI control with code, APIs, tool calls, or program-state operations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>; RecreationWorld and WeaveBench respectively provide environments and benchmarks for such interleaved trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Our previous work, Qwen-UI-Agent, introduced a unified GUI–CLI action space with batched actions, a real-device mobile runtime, and a harness for proactive cross-platform execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. To capture this broader setting, following the system-level perspective of Agents’ Last Exam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we use the term <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalist Computer-Use Agent</span> (GCUA). We define a GCUA as <em id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">an agent system that completes diverse computer tasks through visual interaction and programmatic access, choosing how to observe, act, and organize its work as each task requires</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open Computer-Use Infrastructure.</span> Several projects provide open harnesses for computer-use agents. The <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cua</span> project provides cross-platform desktop drivers and sandbox SDKs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">trycua, 2026</a>)</cite>. The Agent S series provides open orchestration frameworks with memory, visual grounding, and increasingly code-based execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agashe et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agashe et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gonzalez-Pumariega et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Open Computer Use provides an MCP-wrapped desktop-control runtime, including macOS control through Accessibility <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">iFurySt, 2026</a>)</cite>. These are complementary to our work. Open Generalist CUA is an ongoing project to open a full GCUA research stack that couples a trained model with a real-device harness, a cross-device SDK, and execution trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented the first release of Open Generalist CUA, an ongoing project to develop and open the full stack for generalist computer use. Our GCUA formulation centers on three model decisions: what to observe, how to act, and how to organize work. Code execution lets models use intermediate results to coordinate GUI and programmatic operations and apply their existing programming capabilities to computer interaction. Harness-to-model decision transfer complements this design by moving selected observation and task-state decisions from fixed harness rules to the model. On the offline subset of OSWorld-v2, the full OpenGCUA configuration improves task scores across all eight evaluated models, raising mean partial score by 19.6 points and reducing reported steps per task by an average of 53.0% relative to batched GUI-only execution. Ablations show additional benefits from code execution with the same GUI and CLI access, indicating that execution design matters beyond interface access.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We release our implementation, prompts, and evaluation trajectories, alongside preliminary versions of a real-device harness and cross-device SDK. Evaluations on four macOS tasks reveal substantial differences in reported reasoning-token usage and task completion under the same harness. This report studies GCUA designs rather than introducing a newly trained model. OpenGCUA will continue through further research and releases on GCUA models, reasoning and execution efficiency, and real-device environments and benchmarks.</p>
</div>
